WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.388 --> 00:00:17.379
In September the children harvested their Plumfield gardens, and what a bountiful harvest it was!

2
00:00:20.522 --> 00:00:26.326
Demi was able to send boxes of turnips to his grandparents, while Jack had so much corn and

3
00:00:26.366 --> 00:00:28.648
potatoes, he was thinking of selling them.

4
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:35.732
"I'll be glad to buy your potatoes because we go through pounds of that vegetable every week!"

5
00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:36.673
Mr. Bauer said.

6
00:00:38.735 --> 00:00:45.701
Daisy's garden had bloomed from spring to fall, and she regularly sent bouquets to her parents, Meg and

7
00:00:45.761 --> 00:00:46.461
John Brookes.

8
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:54.068
No matter the weather, she faithfully tended her roses, zinnias, and other flowers.

9
00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:04.185
Rob had grown four small squash and one gigantic pumpkin, which was so large two small children could

10
00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:04.906
sit on it.

11
00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.507
Not that he let anyone sit on it!

12
00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:15.289
Instead Rob babied the giant fruit, protecting it from the frost with an old quilt.

13
00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:21.810
"Your pumpkin will provide us with plenty of Thanksgiving pies," Jo said.

14
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:27.012
"I can think of another use for it too, but that's a surprise."

15
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.601
Nan had grown herbs, and her garden had bloomed with useful plants.

16
00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:38.643
Now she was busy cutting and drying her harvest.

17
00:01:40.003 --> 00:01:44.424
She also kept a little book, where she wrote down the uses for different herbs.

18
00:01:44.444 --> 00:01:48.945
"I don't want to poison one of my patients with the wrong herb!"

19
00:01:49.385 --> 00:01:49.906
she said.

20
00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:55.007
Meanwhile Tommy had neglected the beans he'd planted.

21
00:01:56.267 --> 00:02:02.630
When rain was scarce, he hadn't watered them, and then he'd let weeds and pests overtake them.

22
00:02:05.031 --> 00:02:07.593
By midsummer his bean crop had failed.

23
00:02:10.194 --> 00:02:14.457
He'd next planted peas, but it was too late in the season for them to grow.

24
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:21.280
All he succeeded in growing were thistles, which Toby, the donkey, liked to eat.

25
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:27.604
"Next year I'm just going to grow thistles for Toby," Tommy announced.

26
00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:32.218
In contrast, Nat had a bumper crop of beans.

27
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.801
"What a lot of beans you've grown!"

28
00:02:37.262 --> 00:02:40.544
Jo said, admiring his overflowing baskets.

29
00:02:42.045 --> 00:02:47.268
"This was my first garden, and I planted too many beans," Nat said, shaking his head.

30
00:02:47.288 --> 00:02:51.351
"It's going to take me forever to shell all of them!"

31
00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:58.336
"Hmm leave it to me," Jo said with a smile.

32
00:02:59.536 --> 00:03:06.182
Later that day a notice appeared on the barn door, inviting everyone to a dance on Saturday night.

33
00:03:06.202 --> 00:03:14.208
"'Casual attire,'" Nan read the words aloud, "'and take off your shoes at the door.'"

34
00:03:16.149 --> 00:03:19.152
Daisy studied the sign with a disappointed expression.

35
00:03:19.172 --> 00:03:25.017
"I guess that means we can't wear our best dresses," she said.

36
00:03:26.218 --> 00:03:29.000
But her face brightened as she read the rest of the notice.

37
00:03:30.468 --> 00:03:33.511
"Oh, it says Nat is going to play his violin!"

38
00:03:35.513 --> 00:03:39.596
On Saturday night the barn floor was covered with Nat's beans.

39
00:03:39.616 --> 00:03:42.198
"Come on in, everybody!"

40
00:03:42.619 --> 00:03:45.581
he called to his friends, who were taking off their shoes.

41
00:03:49.325 --> 00:03:52.067
Bauer came up with a great idea for shelling my beans.

42
00:03:53.328 --> 00:04:00.408
All you have to do is dance on the pods, and the beans will be shelled—ready?"

43
00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:03.389
The children laughed, and Nat began to play.

44
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.331
The beans were quickly shelled, and everyone helped Nat discard the pods.

45
00:04:11.912 --> 00:04:14.832
Then they danced until it was time for bed.

46
00:04:16.893 --> 00:04:21.994
The only person who did not have a garden was Dan because he'd been away for much of

47
00:04:22.034 --> 00:04:22.494
the summer.

48
00:04:23.835 --> 00:04:28.436
When the others harvested their crops, he looked sad that he had nothing to offer.

49
00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:33.809
But as autumn went on, he thought of a different kind of harvest.

50
00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:41.831
Every Saturday he went off to the forests, fields, and hills and returned with armloads of treasures.

51
00:04:41.851 --> 00:04:44.632
"You bring the woods to me!"

52
00:04:45.032 --> 00:04:51.754
said Jo, who now spent her Saturday afternoons arranging Dan's colorful leaves, grasses, and berries.

53
00:04:51.774 --> 00:04:57.196
"I hope you don't mind, but I put some nuts in the attic," Dan said.

54
00:04:58.468 --> 00:05:03.970
"There are walnuts, chestnuts, and hazelnuts, all in separate boxes that cover half the attic floor."

55
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.431
"Nan's herbs are up there too, hanging to dry.

56
00:05:09.551 --> 00:05:15.613
And Jack is storing corn for popping, while Daisy is putting packets of flower seeds in an old

57
00:05:15.693 --> 00:05:19.114
dresser." Jo laughed.

58
00:05:20.094 --> 00:05:24.996
"We have plenty to be thankful for in the attic, and it's not even Thanksgiving yet!"

59
00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.733
Dan was not the only one gathering nuts.

60
00:05:30.154 --> 00:05:33.896
A huge butternut tree grew on the property, and Mr.

61
00:05:33.956 --> 00:05:36.658
Bauer had promised the nuts to Rob and Teddy.

62
00:05:36.678 --> 00:05:40.960
"I'm going to pick the most nuts!"

63
00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.221
Teddy said one day as he worked.

64
00:05:45.563 --> 00:05:49.005
But he soon grew tired and left his basket on the lawn.

65
00:05:49.025 --> 00:05:52.286
"Have you and Teddy given up?"

66
00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:54.708
Silas asked Rob a few days later.

67
00:05:55.884 --> 00:05:58.945
"The squirrels are busy gathering nuts, and they won't leave you any."

68
00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:03.806
"Oh, Silas, there are plenty of nuts for us," Rob said.

69
00:06:05.487 --> 00:06:10.268
Silas shook his head as he said, "Most of them have fallen, and the squirrels have carried them

70
00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:15.389
away." Rob was alarmed to see that Silas was right.

71
00:06:15.409 --> 00:06:18.830
"Come on, Teddy—we have to pick nuts right now."

72
00:06:22.991 --> 00:06:27.950
The boys worked hard all afternoon while the squirrels chattered at them from the tree.

73
00:06:29.991 --> 00:06:34.094
Right before supper they put the last of the nuts in a little shed.

74
00:06:34.114 --> 00:06:36.195
"There, we're done!"

75
00:06:36.495 --> 00:06:37.136
Rob said.

76
00:06:38.837 --> 00:06:40.898
But the squirrels had not given up.

77
00:06:42.699 --> 00:06:45.802
The next day Silas took the boys out to the shed.

78
00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:52.246
"I noticed your squirrel friends kept dropping onto the roof and disappearing," Silas said.

79
00:06:52.266 --> 00:06:57.225
"Then I saw one poke its head out of a hole—it was carrying one of your nuts."

80
00:06:57.245 --> 00:07:00.466
"They're not squirrel friends!"

81
00:07:00.847 --> 00:07:01.607
Rob cried.

82
00:07:01.627 --> 00:07:04.048
"They're squirrel thieves!"

83
00:07:05.469 --> 00:07:11.412
"I'll help you move the nuts," Silas said, "but you need a better hiding place."

84
00:07:12.392 --> 00:07:13.513
"Up in the attic!"

85
00:07:13.853 --> 00:07:14.553
Teddy said.

86
00:07:14.573 --> 00:07:18.335
"That's where my Danny stores his nuts!"
